<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Zadar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1358">1358</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Zadar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a> was signed on 18 February 1358 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> after a war that Venice had lost. Venice gave up all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kvarner-gulf">Kvarner Gulf</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a>, and the doge dropped the title Duke of Dalmatia. Ragusa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>) passed under loose Hungarian protection and from then on developed as a self-governing republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zadar.jpg" alt="Treaty of Zadar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tiberio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peace-treaty_of_Zadar_1358.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Árpád dynasty died out in 1301, the Angevin dynasty came to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. Its first king, Charles Robert, who ruled from 1312 to 1342, was at first supported by the most powerful Croatian nobleman, Pavao Šubić, Prince of Bribir and Ban of Croatia, who held the coastal cities of Split, Trogir and Šibenik and wielded many of a monarch's powers, from minting coin to taxing towns.</p><p>The Croatian nobles revolted against Pavao and turned to King Charles for help, but in return had to swear direct allegiance to the Hungarian crown. This prepared the way for Hungarian attempts to drive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from the Dalmatian coast, where the towns were caught in a tug of war between the two powers. Ragusa, then held by Venice, was meanwhile growing rich from its position between the West and the mineral-rich kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the 1350s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> gathered a force of 50,000 men, joined by troops of the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the Counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>, Francesco I da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, and the Patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>. In 1356 the coalition besieged the Venetians at Asolo, Conegliano, Ceneda and Treviso, while along the coast it attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a>, Trogir, Split, Ragusa and smaller towns, which mostly surrendered quickly. After a series of defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> accepted unfavourable terms, and the treaty was signed in the cloister of the Monastery of St. Francis in Zadar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> kept the Istrian coast and the region of Treviso, and its naval predominance in the Adriatic survived, since Louis agreed not to build a fleet of his own. Louis entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a> in triumph in 1358, granted wide privileges to its nobility and made it the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>. The treaty marked the rise of the Republic of Ragusa as an independent and successful state. Zadar fared differently: it was later sold back to Venice by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Zadar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Louis I they campaigned widely: in Naples in 1347–1350 to avenge the murder of his brother Andrew, against Venice, which ceded Dalmatia in the <b>Treaty of Zadar</b> in 1358, and in the Balkans, where Bosnia, Serbian lords, Wallachia and Moldavia were drawn into Hungarian suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Venice lost its war against Louis I of Hungary, the <b>Treaty of Zadar</b> of 1358 forced it to give up all of Dalmatia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
